WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.377
- Oh, my God, he will not
let it go. (man laughs)

00:00:02.377 --> 00:00:03.211
You can tell he is Latin,

00:00:03.211 --> 00:00:05.714
because Latin people
are tenacious as (beep)

00:00:06.089 --> 00:00:07.549
My name is Nick Barili

00:00:07.549 --> 00:00:09.259
and I'm traveling the
country to sit down

00:00:09.259 --> 00:00:11.164
with some of the
biggest Latinx artists

00:00:11.164 --> 00:00:12.418
in places that have
meaning to them,

00:00:12.418 --> 00:00:13.560
and help tell their stories.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:15.560
We'll talk about their
journeys and careers 

00:00:15.584 --> 00:00:17.584
and explore issues of
identity, culture,

00:00:17.584 --> 00:00:19.584
discrimination,
and representation.

00:00:19.584 --> 00:00:21.584
This is Seen.

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:25.859
(upbeat music)

00:00:36.327 --> 00:00:38.329
- Good morning, welcome to my hotel room.

00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:39.831
I am in New York city,

00:00:39.831 --> 00:00:41.875
flew across the country from LA.

00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:44.252
We are on our way to meet John Leguizamo.

00:00:44.252 --> 00:00:47.297
Not only is he having a huge
impact on multiple platforms,

00:00:47.297 --> 00:00:50.091
but he also touched my
life in a personal way.

00:00:50.091 --> 00:00:53.636
When I got to see his one
man show as a teenager,

00:00:53.636 --> 00:00:55.472
and really felt seen for the first time.

00:00:55.472 --> 00:00:56.973
Also we're gonna give the man his flowers.

00:00:56.973 --> 00:00:59.893
It's been three decades that
he's been doing it on Broadway,

00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:03.188
on film and TV, and now
writing a comic book.

00:01:03.188 --> 00:01:05.940
So the man does it all
and he does it with humor.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:07.317
And I'm just really excited

00:01:07.317 --> 00:01:08.610
about what we're gonna get to do today.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:09.694
So let's go.

00:01:09.694 --> 00:01:12.280
(upbeat music)

00:01:40.183 --> 00:01:41.017
- You're late.

00:01:41.017 --> 00:01:41.851
- Hello.

00:01:41.851 --> 00:01:42.685
Nick.

00:01:42.685 --> 00:01:43.520
- What's up it's so good to meet you-

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:44.729
- How are you doing,
what took you so long?

00:01:44.729 --> 00:01:46.272
- I'm sorry, New York traffic.

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:47.524
- Yeah New York traffic sucks.

00:01:47.524 --> 00:01:50.193
(upbeat music)

00:01:51.486 --> 00:01:53.279
We're going downstairs to my man cave,

00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:54.405
where the craziness happens.

00:01:54.405 --> 00:01:55.907
(upbeat music)

00:01:55.907 --> 00:01:56.741
- It's a beautiful home you have.

00:01:56.741 --> 00:01:57.575
- Oh, thank you.

00:01:57.575 --> 00:02:01.246
But you say it to everybody,
you meet though. (upbeat music)

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:05.583
- Oh wow.

00:02:05.583 --> 00:02:07.168
I feel like I'm in a
college professor's office

00:02:07.168 --> 00:02:08.795
about to get in trouble.

00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:10.213
(man laughs)

00:02:10.213 --> 00:02:12.882
- Now Nick called you into the office

00:02:12.882 --> 00:02:14.968
because we've heard rumors about you.

00:02:14.968 --> 00:02:15.802
- They're all true.

00:02:15.802 --> 00:02:17.637
- Please share them I'm
ready to take down notes.

00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:18.471
(man laughs)

00:02:18.471 --> 00:02:19.305
I wanna learn from you.

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:21.683
So when you did that
and then what'd you do?

00:02:21.683 --> 00:02:23.601
- I wanna take it back before
we even start the interview.

00:02:23.601 --> 00:02:24.435
- Sure.

00:02:24.435 --> 00:02:26.896
- Because you actually
played a key role in my life.

00:02:26.896 --> 00:02:27.856
- How's that?

00:02:27.856 --> 00:02:30.525
- When I was a senior in
high school in Berkeley,

00:02:30.525 --> 00:02:31.901
my girlfriend at the time was like,

00:02:31.901 --> 00:02:35.155
I wanna go see this show that's
coming to Zellerbach hall.

00:02:35.155 --> 00:02:37.782
And I was like, I can't
afford Zellerbach Hall.

00:02:37.782 --> 00:02:38.616
I tell her oh, it is expensive.

00:02:38.616 --> 00:02:40.076
She was like, "Oh, if we're the ushers,

00:02:40.076 --> 00:02:41.953
we'll get into the show
for free." (man laughs)

00:02:41.953 --> 00:02:43.621
I was like, all right-
- She has a whole system, yeah.

00:02:43.621 --> 00:02:44.455
- I was like all right let's go.

00:02:44.455 --> 00:02:45.248
And it was "Freak.'

00:02:45.248 --> 00:02:46.875
I didn't really know
what I was getting into,

00:02:46.875 --> 00:02:48.793
but I remember sitting down

00:02:48.793 --> 00:02:51.004
and just from the start of your show,

00:02:51.004 --> 00:02:53.214
and this is me as a senior in high school

00:02:53.214 --> 00:02:54.924
was the first time that
I really felt seen.

00:02:54.924 --> 00:02:55.967
It was the first time that I was like,

00:02:55.967 --> 00:02:58.386
wow, there's somebody here
who's got one foot in hip hop,

00:02:58.386 --> 00:03:00.054
one foot in Latin culture.

00:03:00.054 --> 00:03:02.223
Who's going through all
these different things

00:03:02.223 --> 00:03:03.975
with their parents, with immigration.

00:03:03.975 --> 00:03:05.351
Cause I'm a first-generation immigrant.

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:07.687
It was really the first
time where I saw myself

00:03:07.687 --> 00:03:11.858
on stage, on TV, on something
and really felt like,

00:03:11.858 --> 00:03:13.902
okay, I'm not going through this alone.

00:03:13.902 --> 00:03:15.528
And that was the first
time that I was like,

00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:17.614
oh, maybe I can do something in media.

00:03:17.614 --> 00:03:20.158
Maybe I could do something
to represent the journey

00:03:20.158 --> 00:03:20.992
that I'm going through and all the people

00:03:20.992 --> 00:03:23.661
that are going through the
things that I'm going through.

00:03:23.661 --> 00:03:24.495
- Well, that's incredible,

00:03:24.495 --> 00:03:27.332
'cause that's what I
wanted to do with my work.

00:03:27.332 --> 00:03:29.459
I wanted to reach people who are like me,

00:03:29.459 --> 00:03:30.376
kids that were like me,

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:35.131
that felt unseen, unwanted,
that you didn't matter,

00:03:35.131 --> 00:03:36.716
you didn't count,

00:03:36.716 --> 00:03:38.301
cause you have to fight that every day.

00:03:38.301 --> 00:03:40.720
That sense of I can't do this,

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:44.307
this place is not geared for me.

00:03:44.307 --> 00:03:48.478
So, you know I wanted to shake
that up and go, yes you can.

00:03:48.478 --> 00:03:50.104
Every one of us can.

00:03:50.104 --> 00:03:51.689
- I always like to kind
of start interviews

00:03:51.689 --> 00:03:52.732
by setting the tone,

00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:55.026
the way that the intro
sequence in the movie was.

00:03:55.026 --> 00:03:56.986
So if we take it back to Jackson Heights

00:03:56.986 --> 00:03:58.404
to your childhood home,

00:03:58.404 --> 00:04:01.491
that intro sequence like
pans into childhood home,

00:04:01.491 --> 00:04:03.368
what are we seeing? What
are we hearing? (man laughs)

00:04:03.368 --> 00:04:04.202
What are we smelling?

00:04:04.202 --> 00:04:05.036
- Yeah.

00:04:05.036 --> 00:04:06.788
So you're going through Jackson Heights

00:04:06.788 --> 00:04:08.539
and you're coming down

00:04:08.539 --> 00:04:10.041
(man whistling)

00:04:10.041 --> 00:04:13.127
into the planet and then
you see a subway pass by.

00:04:13.127 --> 00:04:14.963
And when the subway clears,

00:04:14.963 --> 00:04:17.465
you keep going through
and there's our apartment

00:04:17.465 --> 00:04:21.886
on the fourth floor 4A of on Deadman
Street. And it was a culdesac

00:04:21.886 --> 00:04:22.720
right next to the subway.

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:25.556
So I would watch a lot of TV

00:04:25.556 --> 00:04:27.725
and maybe this is why I
learned partly how to write

00:04:27.725 --> 00:04:31.229
was because that'd be watching
like Dan August or something.

00:04:31.229 --> 00:04:32.689
When they go, "We just got the killer,"

00:04:32.689 --> 00:04:34.899
and the train would come up chu chu chu

00:04:34.899 --> 00:04:36.693
and you would see this

00:04:36.693 --> 00:04:37.527
(man laughs)

00:04:37.527 --> 00:04:38.987
and that's who the murderer was.

00:04:38.987 --> 00:04:40.822
And I was like, what?

00:04:40.822 --> 00:04:42.824
So I used to have to try
to piece it all together

00:04:42.824 --> 00:04:43.658
in my head.

00:04:43.658 --> 00:04:45.618
And then you would see
my mom cooking I mean,

00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:47.078
who knows pig's feet.

00:04:47.078 --> 00:04:48.621
garbonzos

00:04:48.621 --> 00:04:49.539
you know, rice.

00:04:49.539 --> 00:04:51.040
- What are the sounds that were emanating

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:52.125
from the streets at that time?

00:04:52.125 --> 00:04:55.586
- Yeah sounds were kids
playing, yelling, music,

00:04:55.586 --> 00:04:59.382
music at the time was
always Salsa or some RNB.

00:04:59.382 --> 00:05:02.385
And then Up-rock. Dancing was huge

00:05:02.385 --> 00:05:04.887
at all the community centers afterschool.

00:05:04.887 --> 00:05:06.514
And then when hip hop came, you know,

00:05:06.514 --> 00:05:08.516
all my friends who were like
incredible break-dancers,

00:05:08.516 --> 00:05:09.684
which I couldn't.

00:05:09.684 --> 00:05:12.395
I'd go home and you know,
you practice in your room

00:05:12.395 --> 00:05:13.187
and your mom would go

00:05:13.187 --> 00:05:14.022
(in Spanish)
what are you doing in there?

00:05:14.022 --> 00:05:14.939
what are you doing in there?

00:05:14.939 --> 00:05:16.107
You breaking the walls?

00:05:16.107 --> 00:05:18.526
You're just throwing yourself
around, trying to grab a move.

00:05:18.526 --> 00:05:20.778
But all you're doing is as
bashing against the walls.

00:05:20.778 --> 00:05:23.281
- Talk a little bit about
having that energy growing up,

00:05:23.281 --> 00:05:26.200
I read that you actually got into acting

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:28.036
kind of by getting into trouble at school.

00:05:28.036 --> 00:05:30.455
Can you take us back to that time?

00:05:30.455 --> 00:05:32.165
- You know I was like a class cut up

00:05:32.165 --> 00:05:33.583
kind of like a problematic child.

00:05:33.583 --> 00:05:36.169
I was the poster child for trouble kid.

00:05:36.169 --> 00:05:38.046
The school made me go to therapy

00:05:38.046 --> 00:05:40.131
'cause they wouldn't let me
back unless I had therapy,

00:05:40.131 --> 00:05:42.508
which I hated at the moment.

00:05:42.508 --> 00:05:44.969
But then now, you know, it saved my life.

00:05:44.969 --> 00:05:47.096
But one of my friends
Indio suggested like,

00:05:47.096 --> 00:05:50.016
why don't we commandeer
the number seven train

00:05:50.016 --> 00:05:51.059
and you get on the mic.

00:05:51.059 --> 00:05:52.226
It was like an open mic.

00:05:52.226 --> 00:05:55.730
He made up his own open
mic night on the subway.

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:57.565
So we kicked the conductor's booth open,

00:05:57.565 --> 00:06:00.109
and then I grabbed the mic
and I started, you know,

00:06:00.109 --> 00:06:01.235
(man laughs)

00:06:01.235 --> 00:06:02.153
doing all my voices

00:06:02.153 --> 00:06:03.863
(man mumbling)

00:06:03.863 --> 00:06:07.158
I'll say boy I said, boy,
you're a chicken hog boy.

00:06:07.158 --> 00:06:08.242
- Over the speaker for the whole?

00:06:08.242 --> 00:06:09.077
- Yes.

00:06:09.077 --> 00:06:09.911
- Wow.

00:06:09.911 --> 00:06:12.038
- And I kept going and
going and singing songs

00:06:12.038 --> 00:06:12.872
and whatever.

00:06:12.872 --> 00:06:16.209
- How did you go from that to
actual having acting classes?

00:06:16.209 --> 00:06:19.462
- Well, my math teacher,
Mr.Uzufa would tell me,

00:06:19.462 --> 00:06:21.714
instead of, you know, Mr. Leguizamo

00:06:21.714 --> 00:06:22.799
instead of wrecking the class,

00:06:22.799 --> 00:06:24.300
they can make penicillin from moldy bread.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:25.802
They can make something out of you.

00:06:25.802 --> 00:06:27.011
Why don't you take your,

00:06:27.011 --> 00:06:29.180
whatever it is that you're
doing become a comedian?

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:31.307
So it was like the first person that said,

00:06:31.307 --> 00:06:34.060
oh wow I could maybe
do something with this.

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:35.269
And he was a mentor, you know?

00:06:35.269 --> 00:06:36.854
Mr. Uzufa was a mentor.

00:06:36.854 --> 00:06:39.690
You need like, when you
come from the neighborhoods

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:41.025
that we come from,

00:06:41.025 --> 00:06:44.987
you need somebody outside of
you to tell you that you can,

00:06:44.987 --> 00:06:45.822
that you're worthy.

00:06:45.822 --> 00:06:47.865
So I needed that several times.

00:06:47.865 --> 00:06:49.158
So yeah I found an acting school

00:06:49.158 --> 00:06:50.701
Sylvia Leigh Showcase Theater.

00:06:50.701 --> 00:06:53.204
And I went there and this
little old lady who looked,

00:06:53.204 --> 00:06:54.455
sounded like Katharine Hepburn's was like,

00:06:54.455 --> 00:06:55.957
"Oh, it's so good to have you.

00:06:55.957 --> 00:06:57.625
Ah ah."

00:06:57.625 --> 00:06:59.085
And then I, you know, I
say, what was that lady?

00:06:59.085 --> 00:06:59.919
What you do?

00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:01.254
She's like, "Oh, your accent is horrific,

00:07:01.254 --> 00:07:03.464
we're going to have to do something.

00:07:03.464 --> 00:07:05.675
You'll never work with that sound."

00:07:05.675 --> 00:07:06.592
What's up lady?

00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:08.261
Why not? And you know, so she gave me,

00:07:08.261 --> 00:07:10.763
she sold me like hundreds of dollars worth

00:07:10.763 --> 00:07:13.182
of like dialect classes.

00:07:13.182 --> 00:07:14.976
So she was making a buck off my back,

00:07:14.976 --> 00:07:17.395
but you know, that's why I
sound so incredible today.

00:07:17.395 --> 00:07:19.439
So you can't tell where I'm from.

00:07:19.439 --> 00:07:21.732
You thought I was from England,
didn't you? (man laughs)

00:07:21.732 --> 00:07:22.692
Why are you laughing like that?

00:07:22.692 --> 00:07:24.819
- 'Cause I heard the same thing.

00:07:24.819 --> 00:07:27.405
Like I originally want to
be a sports broadcaster,

00:07:27.405 --> 00:07:29.699
and people were like, you'll
never be a sports broadcaster,

00:07:29.699 --> 00:07:30.533
you got a thick accent.

00:07:30.533 --> 00:07:32.618
Like that's not, oh,
and you have, you know,

00:07:32.618 --> 00:07:34.620
you slow down your speech and sometimes,

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:36.330
and they're like, you'll
never be a sport broadcaster.

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:37.165
- How crazy is that,

00:07:37.165 --> 00:07:39.417
cause that's the sound that I wanna hear.

00:07:39.417 --> 00:07:42.086
I'm like, where are the
Latinx sports casters?

00:07:42.086 --> 00:07:43.546
I wanna hear those sounds.

00:07:43.546 --> 00:07:44.380
- Yeah.

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:46.174
- I was talking to a
couple of friends recently,

00:07:46.174 --> 00:07:48.384
we're doing a movie and there
were three Latin people in it,

00:07:48.384 --> 00:07:49.427
which was like a miracle

00:07:49.427 --> 00:07:51.679
because it's, I'm always the only one,

00:07:51.679 --> 00:07:52.805
and they're the only ones.

00:07:52.805 --> 00:07:55.516
This one actress had a much thicker accent

00:07:55.516 --> 00:07:57.894
because she's younger, so she's
had less time to work on it.

00:07:57.894 --> 00:07:59.562
But I go, I love your sound.

00:07:59.562 --> 00:08:01.230
You know, I love Rosie Perez's sound.

00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:03.649
I love Dascha Polanco's sound.

00:08:03.649 --> 00:08:07.403
I love those the way
that the language moves,

00:08:07.403 --> 00:08:10.865
the dip thongs is stretched
out and little bit of urban,

00:08:10.865 --> 00:08:13.242
a little Latino in it is
such a beautiful sound.

00:08:13.242 --> 00:08:14.076
- Yep.

00:08:14.076 --> 00:08:17.288
Once you started to act,
did that feel like a way

00:08:17.288 --> 00:08:19.415
to just work through some of that energy?

00:08:19.415 --> 00:08:20.833
- I think it's a combination of things.

00:08:20.833 --> 00:08:23.836
I mean, definitely in that acting class,

00:08:23.836 --> 00:08:25.254
the teacher was brilliant actually.

00:08:25.254 --> 00:08:26.547
And she gave me "Dino" this book,

00:08:26.547 --> 00:08:28.424
it's about a teenager
who's having troubles

00:08:28.424 --> 00:08:30.593
at home with his, hates his father

00:08:30.593 --> 00:08:31.427
(man laughs)

00:08:31.427 --> 00:08:33.888
and was arrested and is in therapy.

00:08:33.888 --> 00:08:36.390
And it was like, oh wait,
check, check, check, check.

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:38.768
So when I did it, it was like an epiphany.

00:08:38.768 --> 00:08:41.103
But that's when I realized,
oh my God, I can do this.

00:08:41.103 --> 00:08:42.688
This is only thing I wanna do.

00:08:42.688 --> 00:08:43.523
I don't wanna...

00:08:43.523 --> 00:08:45.525
I don't care if I'm famous,
or successful, or rich,

00:08:45.525 --> 00:08:47.902
I just wanna do this thing.

00:08:47.902 --> 00:08:50.696
- I think there's a couple of
years before you get to NYU,

00:08:50.696 --> 00:08:52.073
what are those years like?

00:08:52.073 --> 00:08:54.534
And is that when you're working at KFC?

00:08:54.534 --> 00:08:56.577
- KFC was paying for my acting classes.

00:08:56.577 --> 00:09:00.581
Cause her diction
classes and diction tapes

00:09:00.581 --> 00:09:02.124
were mad expensive.

00:09:02.124 --> 00:09:04.293
So yeah I worked there for two years.

00:09:04.293 --> 00:09:06.754
At the Times Square KFC, I saw things

00:09:06.754 --> 00:09:08.422
that no young man should ever see.

00:09:08.422 --> 00:09:10.132
- Walk me through that
period in your life.

00:09:10.132 --> 00:09:12.843
I mean, you're working
at KFC, you're acting,

00:09:12.843 --> 00:09:13.803
you're going to school.

00:09:13.803 --> 00:09:15.471
- Yeah.
- Like what was that time like?

00:09:15.471 --> 00:09:16.514
We see now we see all the awards,

00:09:16.514 --> 00:09:18.224
we see this amazing office you have,

00:09:18.224 --> 00:09:20.142
but it's like, I'm sure
there's somebody watching

00:09:20.142 --> 00:09:23.062
who is probably in those shoes
when you were at that time,

00:09:23.062 --> 00:09:25.064
figuring how am I gonna
make it out of this job?

00:09:25.064 --> 00:09:27.692
And to start my career.

00:09:27.692 --> 00:09:29.110
- I was failing in high school,

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:30.695
but I was doing the acting classes.

00:09:30.695 --> 00:09:32.446
I was in therapy.

00:09:32.446 --> 00:09:35.533
And then, you know, reality hit.

00:09:35.533 --> 00:09:37.952
I was like, oh my God, I
gotta apply for colleges.

00:09:37.952 --> 00:09:40.371
My grade point was terrible and

00:09:40.371 --> 00:09:41.539
(in Spanish)
How do you say in Spanish, 

00:09:41.539 --> 00:09:42.540
(in Spanish)
I got my act together?

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:46.377
I got sharp and I was
like, I gotta fix myself.

00:09:46.377 --> 00:09:47.503
I gotta fix my life.

00:09:47.503 --> 00:09:48.838
- What inspired you to.

00:09:48.838 --> 00:09:50.339
(in Spanish)
“To get your act together."

00:09:50.339 --> 00:09:51.966
- Working at Kentucky Fried Chicken,

00:09:51.966 --> 00:09:53.467
you know, I just saw what I'm not.

00:09:53.467 --> 00:09:56.512
I did it all, I was cook, I was cashier,

00:09:56.512 --> 00:09:57.680
I did the cater, I did it all.

00:09:57.680 --> 00:09:59.557
I go, this is gonna be
the rest of my life.

00:09:59.557 --> 00:10:00.891
I don't want that to be me.

00:10:00.891 --> 00:10:02.059
I wanna have something.

00:10:02.059 --> 00:10:04.228
And then I couldn't get into NYU at first.

00:10:04.228 --> 00:10:05.313
I had to go to C.W. Post,

00:10:05.313 --> 00:10:07.356
which actually ended up
being the most education

00:10:07.356 --> 00:10:09.025
I ever got in my entire
life with those two years,

00:10:09.025 --> 00:10:10.401
I spent at CW post.

00:10:10.401 --> 00:10:11.444
And then I transferred to NYU

00:10:11.444 --> 00:10:14.572
because that was the best acting program

00:10:14.572 --> 00:10:17.158
that I thought existed in America.

00:10:17.158 --> 00:10:20.369
Here I am in NYU, A student.

00:10:20.369 --> 00:10:22.538
I had fixed my accent a lot by this point.

00:10:22.538 --> 00:10:25.041
And all the white kids in my class

00:10:25.041 --> 00:10:28.127
were going to five auditions a day.

00:10:28.127 --> 00:10:31.339
I was going to one every
five months and I was like,

00:10:31.339 --> 00:10:33.591
wait a minute, I'm working
as hard as they are,

00:10:33.591 --> 00:10:35.134
I got better grades.

00:10:35.134 --> 00:10:36.177
And then I realized, oh my God,

00:10:36.177 --> 00:10:39.764
I don't have the same
opportunities as they have.

00:10:39.764 --> 00:10:42.308
And I realized, I know I realized

00:10:42.308 --> 00:10:44.685
that it wasn't an equal playing field.

00:10:44.685 --> 00:10:46.520
You know, it just was never gonna be.

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:48.606
You're a disillusioned, you're
a young man and you realize,

00:10:48.606 --> 00:10:49.940
oh my God, life is not fair.

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:54.320
Just because of how I look, how I sound,

00:10:55.237 --> 00:10:57.406
my economic class that I come from,

00:10:57.406 --> 00:10:58.908
it's just not a fair playing field,

00:10:58.908 --> 00:11:00.910
no matter how talented you
are, it doesn't matter.

00:11:00.910 --> 00:11:02.745
And, but I thought talent-

00:11:02.745 --> 00:11:03.871
- [Interviewer] Was the equalizer.

00:11:03.871 --> 00:11:05.373
- It was the great equalizer.

00:11:05.373 --> 00:11:09.293
And so the audition that I
would go to every five months

00:11:09.293 --> 00:11:13.756
was always for a drug
dealer, a murderer, a killer,

00:11:13.756 --> 00:11:18.260
or somebody, your gardener or
somebody servicing your house.

00:11:18.260 --> 00:11:19.595
And it was like, wait a minute,

00:11:19.595 --> 00:11:22.932
I've worked this hard to be
playing those kinds of roles?

00:11:22.932 --> 00:11:24.975
I started doing improv and other things

00:11:24.975 --> 00:11:28.979
cause I was like this
working towards Hollywood,

00:11:28.979 --> 00:11:31.315
which was really Holly-wouldn't

00:11:31.315 --> 00:11:33.025
was not gonna be the place.

00:11:33.025 --> 00:11:35.903
So I've start to find
other ways other avenues.

00:11:35.903 --> 00:11:38.489
And I started finding
theater, Off-Broadway theater

00:11:38.489 --> 00:11:39.532
and performance art,

00:11:39.532 --> 00:11:43.661
and performance art was the
place that I found my freedom.

00:11:43.661 --> 00:11:45.871
- Was that your way of
just showing Hollywood

00:11:45.871 --> 00:11:47.707
your own worth through your own eyes,

00:11:47.707 --> 00:11:48.541
not through their eyes?

00:11:48.541 --> 00:11:49.375
- Yes, yes.

00:11:49.375 --> 00:11:52.712
Writing became my way to,

00:11:52.712 --> 00:11:55.506
for me as an artist to be my full self

00:11:55.506 --> 00:11:58.134
and how I saw myself, not how Hollywood

00:11:58.134 --> 00:12:01.303
or Network TV saw me.

00:12:01.303 --> 00:12:04.765
Cause look right now, the
census came out, the 2020 Census

00:12:04.765 --> 00:12:07.476
and said almost 20% of the population.

00:12:07.476 --> 00:12:08.978
And then a couple of years ago,

00:12:08.978 --> 00:12:12.022
we were only 3% of the faces
in front of the camera,

00:12:12.022 --> 00:12:15.818
less than 1% of the stories,
less than 1% of the crew,

00:12:15.818 --> 00:12:20.823
less than 1% of the executives.
That's cultural apartheid.

00:12:21.782 --> 00:12:24.034
And the same thing goes for politics,

00:12:24.034 --> 00:12:26.370
less than 1% of elected officials

00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:28.080
and forget about publication,

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:31.333
Latin children are least seen
in children's picture books.

00:12:31.333 --> 00:12:32.752
So right then there,

00:12:32.752 --> 00:12:36.088
your self esteem as a child
is already being challenged.

00:12:38.215 --> 00:12:39.091
Think about that,

00:12:40.342 --> 00:12:41.802
marinate on that.

00:12:41.802 --> 00:12:43.053
I'll be right back.

00:12:43.053 --> 00:12:44.013
(man laughs)

00:12:44.013 --> 00:12:46.599
Thank you for joining us in
my podcast with Nick and John.

00:12:46.599 --> 00:12:49.226
- The people who might
not be of the culture

00:12:49.226 --> 00:12:52.396
might not fully understand the
full impact of that, right?

00:12:52.396 --> 00:12:56.734
Because that not only
affects our self-esteem,

00:12:56.734 --> 00:12:59.320
it also affects how
people who don't interact

00:12:59.320 --> 00:13:01.071
with us on a daily basis, see us.

00:13:01.071 --> 00:13:02.072
- See us.

00:13:02.072 --> 00:13:02.907
- Then they don't necessarily think

00:13:02.907 --> 00:13:04.450
about our positive
contributions to this country.

00:13:04.450 --> 00:13:06.535
- Right. So not only are we invisible,

00:13:06.535 --> 00:13:09.413
but when we are seen it's
a negative portrayal.

00:13:09.413 --> 00:13:12.875
- You said in an interview,
it was in 94, it was on PBS.

00:13:12.875 --> 00:13:14.627
And you said that most of the roles

00:13:14.627 --> 00:13:16.670
that you were up for were stereotypical

00:13:16.670 --> 00:13:19.340
and were one dimensional,
and you were just asking

00:13:19.340 --> 00:13:21.217
to have three dimensional characters

00:13:21.217 --> 00:13:24.220
who could actually lead a movie
and not just be supporting.

00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:28.933
- Right how do you create
a Latin star in America,

00:13:28.933 --> 00:13:33.437
when the roles are one dimensional
and not worthy of awards?

00:13:33.437 --> 00:13:36.982
The ugly question is why
aren't Latin people succeeding?

00:13:36.982 --> 00:13:38.192
What's the ugly question?

00:13:38.192 --> 00:13:39.276
Are we not smart enough?

00:13:39.276 --> 00:13:40.110
Not talented enough?

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:41.737
Not good looking enough?

00:13:41.737 --> 00:13:43.322
Not hardworking enough?

00:13:43.322 --> 00:13:46.492
No, none of those
stereotypes and racist ideas.

00:13:46.492 --> 00:13:48.828
Cause nobody tries
harder with less access.

00:13:48.828 --> 00:13:50.996
A friend of mine, I won't say her name,

00:13:50.996 --> 00:13:54.834
years ago when I was doing
Mambo Mouth sent out her resume.

00:13:54.834 --> 00:13:59.088
Dark-skinned Latin woman sent
out her resume with a picture,

00:13:59.088 --> 00:14:00.631
one with a Latin name, her real name,

00:14:00.631 --> 00:14:02.925
and then one with a white-fied name.

00:14:02.925 --> 00:14:07.388
The white-fied got callbacks,
the Latin one got nothing.

00:14:07.388 --> 00:14:08.222
That's just-

00:14:08.222 --> 00:14:09.431
- Just off a name.

00:14:09.431 --> 00:14:10.975
- Just off a name exactly.

00:14:10.975 --> 00:14:13.644
And that's just explains
the whole situation

00:14:13.644 --> 00:14:14.770
right then and there.

00:14:14.770 --> 00:14:16.188
- You made that statement in 94

00:14:16.188 --> 00:14:20.442
where, you know, almost 30 years later,

00:14:20.442 --> 00:14:22.111
do you feel like things have changed?

00:14:22.111 --> 00:14:23.362
- Yes, yes.

00:14:23.362 --> 00:14:25.406
Things have changed
incrementally unfortunately.

00:14:25.406 --> 00:14:28.117
40 years I've been in this business

00:14:28.117 --> 00:14:32.288
and I guess we went from
1% to maybe this year,

00:14:32.288 --> 00:14:35.124
I think we're gonna be at four or 5%

00:14:35.124 --> 00:14:35.958
in front of the camera,

00:14:35.958 --> 00:14:38.752
I'm not sure what the numbers are behind.

00:14:38.752 --> 00:14:40.337
I mean, things are improving.

00:14:40.337 --> 00:14:45.301
I think COVID made us really
look at ourselves in America.

00:14:45.301 --> 00:14:49.221
I think Black Lives Matter was
huge awakening for America,

00:14:49.221 --> 00:14:51.473
a reboot for America to look at themselves

00:14:51.473 --> 00:14:53.475
and see what's going on.

00:14:53.475 --> 00:14:55.519
I think everybody's trying
to do the right thing

00:14:55.519 --> 00:14:58.480
and hire many more people of color.

00:14:58.480 --> 00:14:59.773
I mean, what I wanna see,

00:14:59.773 --> 00:15:04.737
I want to see 20% of the
roles in front of the camera,

00:15:04.778 --> 00:15:07.865
of the crew, of the
stories, of the executives.

00:15:07.865 --> 00:15:09.992
We're 25% at the US box office.

00:15:09.992 --> 00:15:11.201
- Yeah one in four.

00:15:11.201 --> 00:15:13.996
- And we have 3% of the faces?

00:15:13.996 --> 00:15:17.917
I'm not asking for extra
I just want what's due us.

00:15:17.917 --> 00:15:19.627
I mean, I've seen the development process

00:15:19.627 --> 00:15:22.504
through all my life at
every studio, New Line,

00:15:22.504 --> 00:15:23.589
Universal, Fox Searchlight.

00:15:23.589 --> 00:15:25.799
They work on scripts for years and years,

00:15:25.799 --> 00:15:27.509
polishing them, making it better.

00:15:27.509 --> 00:15:28.844
And half of those die

00:15:28.844 --> 00:15:30.804
and then half of them
make it to the screen.

00:15:30.804 --> 00:15:32.139
We need to be in that pipeline.

00:15:32.139 --> 00:15:34.725
They need to be taking Latin scripts

00:15:34.725 --> 00:15:36.101
and working them that hard,

00:15:36.101 --> 00:15:37.645
so by the time they get to this point,

00:15:37.645 --> 00:15:39.855
they're the best that can be.

00:15:39.855 --> 00:15:40.689
- Yeah.

00:15:40.689 --> 00:15:42.483
- But it just takes
somebody who looks like me

00:15:42.483 --> 00:15:44.568
being an executive and
saying that story is worth it

00:15:44.568 --> 00:15:46.570
cause I've been pitching
stories for 30 years,

00:15:46.570 --> 00:15:49.823
Always thinking that my
writing was falling short

00:15:49.823 --> 00:15:50.866
because they never got green-lit.

00:15:50.866 --> 00:15:52.910
So I was like, damn I
thought it was a good script.

00:15:52.910 --> 00:15:55.621
But then I'm winning awards
on Broadway and off-Broadway

00:15:55.621 --> 00:15:58.874
Obie awards and Drama
Desk Awards for my writing

00:15:58.874 --> 00:16:03.420
and a Tony, Tony
nominations and even Emmy's,

00:16:03.420 --> 00:16:06.340
but never getting my movies done.

00:16:06.340 --> 00:16:09.635
And you know, there was
always excuses were white,

00:16:09.635 --> 00:16:11.553
Latin people don't wanna see Latin people.

00:16:11.553 --> 00:16:12.388
I'm like, no,

00:16:12.388 --> 00:16:15.474
then who do I wanna see
for Christmas at my house?

00:16:15.474 --> 00:16:16.976
Some Norwegian family?

00:16:16.976 --> 00:16:19.728
I wanna see Latin people
is what I wanna see.

00:16:19.728 --> 00:16:21.605
Or they would tell me like
when Critical Thinking,

00:16:21.605 --> 00:16:23.357
I directed, they would tell me,

00:16:23.357 --> 00:16:25.609
"Latin people don't wanna
see feel good movies."

00:16:25.609 --> 00:16:28.070
No, no we wanna see really
depressing suicidal flicks.

00:16:28.070 --> 00:16:28.904
That's-

00:16:28.904 --> 00:16:29.738
- That's an actual conversation?

00:16:29.738 --> 00:16:30.781
- [John] Yeah actual thing.

00:16:30.781 --> 00:16:32.950
- That's insane that that
would come out of someone.

00:16:32.950 --> 00:16:35.035
- Was it, it's Hollywood wisdom.

00:16:35.035 --> 00:16:36.537
There is an audience and a hunger,

00:16:36.537 --> 00:16:39.873
I know that exists regardless
of what a studio head

00:16:39.873 --> 00:16:41.542
or network says to me anymore.

00:16:41.542 --> 00:16:46.005
I know Hamilton would have
never got made at a studio

00:16:46.005 --> 00:16:47.423
or a network.

00:16:47.423 --> 00:16:49.008
They would have been, "Oh I'm sorry, Lynn,

00:16:49.008 --> 00:16:51.135
I'm sorry but wait a minute,

00:16:51.135 --> 00:16:54.054
Burr's gonna be black and
Hamilton's gonna be Puerto Rican?

00:16:54.054 --> 00:16:55.514
I'm so sorry, I didn't mean to laugh,

00:16:55.514 --> 00:16:58.100
but I can assure they
didn't speak in hip hop

00:16:58.100 --> 00:17:00.227
back in the 1700s the founding father."

00:17:00.227 --> 00:17:02.438
It would have never gotten made, never.

00:17:02.438 --> 00:17:06.442
But theater, there are no gatekeepers.

00:17:06.442 --> 00:17:08.485
You just gotta raise that
money and get a good review,

00:17:08.485 --> 00:17:10.696
and now you've got the biggest masterpiece

00:17:10.696 --> 00:17:12.448
that Broadway has ever seen.

00:17:12.448 --> 00:17:15.117
(upbeat music)

00:17:19.455 --> 00:17:20.664
- I have a bunch more questions,

00:17:20.664 --> 00:17:22.624
but how do you wanna space it between here

00:17:22.624 --> 00:17:23.459
and the restaurant?

00:17:23.459 --> 00:17:24.293
- Oh, he's hungry.

00:17:24.293 --> 00:17:25.753
That's his segue.

00:17:25.753 --> 00:17:26.587
(men laughs)

00:17:26.587 --> 00:17:28.589
That's his subtle way
of saying I'm hungry.

00:17:28.589 --> 00:17:31.258
(upbeat music)

00:17:34.678 --> 00:17:35.512
'Cause your stomach was grumbling

00:17:35.512 --> 00:17:36.972
I'm gonna take you to lunch.

00:17:36.972 --> 00:17:38.849
- Yeah, is this one of
your favorites spots?

00:17:38.849 --> 00:17:40.893
- One of my favorite spots,
the margarita's are killer.

00:17:40.893 --> 00:17:43.187
(upbeat music)

00:17:43.187 --> 00:17:45.230
Down here I mean, I used
to go to a lot of theater

00:17:45.230 --> 00:17:46.065
and perform.

00:17:46.065 --> 00:17:47.566
That's where you would ask
me about where did I perform?

00:17:47.566 --> 00:17:50.277
It was, I did my
performance art at PS 122,

00:17:50.277 --> 00:17:54.281
a converted school,
Gusto House, Dixon Place,

00:17:54.281 --> 00:17:56.075
The Kitchen, Knitting Factory,

00:17:56.075 --> 00:17:59.119
all these little spots like
he had like 20 people maybe,

00:17:59.119 --> 00:17:59.953
(man laughs)

00:17:59.953 --> 00:18:00.913
in the audience.

00:18:00.913 --> 00:18:01.997
- Do you remember that first time

00:18:01.997 --> 00:18:03.832
that you hit the stage
what that feeling was like?

00:18:03.832 --> 00:18:04.875
- Oh my God, it was incredible.

00:18:04.875 --> 00:18:07.377
I was doing a one-man piece.

00:18:07.377 --> 00:18:10.047
It was a one-man show about Latin people

00:18:10.047 --> 00:18:13.092
had written the Bible, as Jesus, and Mary

00:18:13.092 --> 00:18:17.179
and all Latinos, you know,
loud and boisterous and fun.

00:18:17.179 --> 00:18:18.514
And the crowd loved it.

00:18:18.514 --> 00:18:19.348
And it was amazing.

00:18:19.348 --> 00:18:22.601
It was my first full
length piece that I wrote

00:18:22.601 --> 00:18:24.686
and performed by myself,
playing all the characters.

00:18:24.686 --> 00:18:25.813
- Were you nervous or?

00:18:25.813 --> 00:18:26.647
- Oh hell yeah.

00:18:26.647 --> 00:18:28.273
If you're not nervous, then you're not,

00:18:28.273 --> 00:18:31.360
then it's not really, you're
not really risking anything.

00:18:31.360 --> 00:18:34.029
(upbeat music)

00:18:34.029 --> 00:18:35.447
Here we are it's my spot right here.

00:18:35.447 --> 00:18:37.282
(upbeat music)

00:18:37.282 --> 00:18:38.534
You have your vax card?

00:18:38.534 --> 00:18:39.868
- Yup.

00:18:39.868 --> 00:18:40.702
- Hey, how you doing?

00:18:40.702 --> 00:18:41.662
How's it going?

00:18:41.662 --> 00:18:43.330
How are you?

00:18:43.330 --> 00:18:45.999
(upbeat music)

00:18:47.459 --> 00:18:49.211
How about a little mescal?

00:18:49.211 --> 00:18:50.295
- I'm down, what are you drinking?

00:18:50.295 --> 00:18:51.463
- A little Oaxaca express.

00:18:51.463 --> 00:18:53.132
- [Waiter] Oaxaca express?

00:18:53.132 --> 00:18:53.966
- Yeah.

00:18:53.966 --> 00:18:56.552
(upbeat music)

00:18:57.678 --> 00:18:59.096
Yeah.

00:18:59.096 --> 00:19:00.848
Oh, here comes, Here's lunch.

00:19:00.848 --> 00:19:01.682
(man laughs)

00:19:01.682 --> 00:19:02.516
- Two Oaxaca express.

00:19:02.516 --> 00:19:03.433
- Thank you.

00:19:03.433 --> 00:19:04.393
- Thank you.

00:19:04.393 --> 00:19:05.227
Thank you brother.

00:19:05.227 --> 00:19:06.061
- Appreciate it.

00:19:06.061 --> 00:19:06.895
- It's a meal replacement here we go.

00:19:06.895 --> 00:19:07.729
- Cheers.

00:19:07.729 --> 00:19:08.730
- Cheers brother, heres to you.

00:19:08.730 --> 00:19:09.773
- To you.

00:19:09.773 --> 00:19:12.067
To all the doors you've opened.

00:19:12.067 --> 00:19:13.735
- And the glass ceiling, that keeps...

00:19:13.735 --> 00:19:16.113
I got scars in my head from
hitting that glass ceiling.

00:19:16.113 --> 00:19:18.365
Luckily I'm hardheaded so
I don't really feel it.

00:19:18.365 --> 00:19:20.409
- I feel like we have
to be in this industry

00:19:20.409 --> 00:19:23.537
just to be able to make
it past all the no's.

00:19:23.537 --> 00:19:24.371
- Yes.

00:19:24.371 --> 00:19:25.205
- Talk about that a little bit.

00:19:25.205 --> 00:19:28.000
Cause I feel like you hear so
many no's that like it's hard,

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:31.211
I don't care how hard your skin is.

00:19:31.211 --> 00:19:32.045
- Yeah.

00:19:32.045 --> 00:19:33.589
- At some point you start
to internalize that.

00:19:33.589 --> 00:19:35.716
Like how do you maneuver
through the industry

00:19:35.716 --> 00:19:37.176
and all the no's?

00:19:37.176 --> 00:19:39.303
- It's interesting because
you know, the rule is,

00:19:39.303 --> 00:19:42.764
you become a celebrity, you get
a certain amount of success,

00:19:42.764 --> 00:19:45.142
you don't talk about your problems.

00:19:45.142 --> 00:19:47.186
You don't talk about the difficulties.

00:19:47.186 --> 00:19:51.565
You act like, oh it was a
magic carpet ride you know,

00:19:51.565 --> 00:19:52.816
I feel like it's really important

00:19:52.816 --> 00:19:55.819
to talk about all the problems
and all the difficulties,

00:19:55.819 --> 00:19:58.238
especially if you're a person of color,

00:19:58.238 --> 00:19:59.615
and especially if you're Latinx,

00:19:59.615 --> 00:20:01.241
there's a lot of the people struggling

00:20:01.241 --> 00:20:04.161
and we need to change things.

00:20:04.161 --> 00:20:06.288
And Spike Lee showed me
cause he's one of the first

00:20:06.288 --> 00:20:08.290
who spoke out against #Hollywoodsowhite.

00:20:09.458 --> 00:20:12.586
Well, he was #Oscarsowhite,
but I wasn't sure

00:20:12.586 --> 00:20:13.629
(man laughs)

00:20:13.629 --> 00:20:16.256
if you're okay, so let's be real.

00:20:16.256 --> 00:20:19.176
So he brought that out
and I was like, yeah,

00:20:19.176 --> 00:20:21.386
I went right and started
re-tweeting him like crazy.

00:20:21.386 --> 00:20:23.388
And then I had posted #Oscarsowhite,

00:20:24.348 --> 00:20:27.184
but #Hollywoodsevenwhiter.

00:20:27.184 --> 00:20:29.478
You know, there's colorism
within Latin culture

00:20:29.478 --> 00:20:30.312
that we have to fix,

00:20:30.312 --> 00:20:33.065
but there's colorism in Hollywood too.

00:20:33.065 --> 00:20:35.317
I mean, I'd benefited
from being light-skinned,

00:20:35.317 --> 00:20:37.611
and I stayed out of the
sun so I could work.

00:20:37.611 --> 00:20:38.612
- [Nick] Wow.

00:20:38.612 --> 00:20:40.239
- I definitely would not
go in the sun for years.

00:20:40.239 --> 00:20:41.990
I was so pasty-
- That was a conscious thing?

00:20:41.990 --> 00:20:43.825
- Oh yeah, so I could work.

00:20:43.825 --> 00:20:46.161
And all the Latinos that made it so far,

00:20:46.161 --> 00:20:48.413
a lot of them were all
light-skinned you know,

00:20:48.413 --> 00:20:51.416
what happened to all the Afro-Latino

00:20:51.416 --> 00:20:53.877
and the majority of indigenous Latinos,

00:20:53.877 --> 00:20:56.380
they don't get a shot, you know?

00:20:56.380 --> 00:20:59.091
So there's a lot of things we
got to deal with in Hollywood,

00:20:59.091 --> 00:21:00.092
and we gotta fix them,

00:21:00.092 --> 00:21:02.552
we gotta speak out and we got to speak up

00:21:02.552 --> 00:21:03.595
and you're doing it.

00:21:03.595 --> 00:21:08.058
I mean, this is such an important
space to have, you know,

00:21:08.058 --> 00:21:10.018
to be able to talk about all these things.

00:21:10.018 --> 00:21:15.023
- You have been not shy about
vocalizing these things.

00:21:15.524 --> 00:21:16.692
Why is it important to you?

00:21:16.692 --> 00:21:18.402
And why do you think not as many people

00:21:18.402 --> 00:21:20.362
are as vocal as you
are about this subject?

00:21:20.362 --> 00:21:22.572
- Well, being vocal has its cost.

00:21:22.572 --> 00:21:25.826
It has a cost like when I became
very politically conscious

00:21:25.826 --> 00:21:29.746
and then politically on my
social, I lost half my followers.

00:21:29.746 --> 00:21:30.831
- [Nick] Wow.

00:21:30.831 --> 00:21:33.834
- And then I got a lot of hate tweets,

00:21:33.834 --> 00:21:34.876
"Go back to your country,"

00:21:34.876 --> 00:21:36.336
"Go back to Mexico,"

00:21:36.336 --> 00:21:39.381
which I'm not Mexican, but
I'll gladly go back to Mexico.

00:21:39.381 --> 00:21:40.632
Cause it's a great country.

00:21:40.632 --> 00:21:42.926
Still like when I post
political stuff on Facebook,

00:21:42.926 --> 00:21:45.178
they go, "John used to be so entertaining,

00:21:45.178 --> 00:21:46.096
now you're a bore"

00:21:46.096 --> 00:21:49.349
but you know, all this hate
stuff, which I ignore, you know.

00:21:49.349 --> 00:21:52.519
I feel like if you've achieved
a certain amount of success,

00:21:52.519 --> 00:21:54.479
it's your duty to give back.

00:21:54.479 --> 00:21:55.814
You gotta give back.

00:21:55.814 --> 00:21:58.442
You can't be an ostrich and
stick your head in the sand

00:21:58.442 --> 00:22:00.569
and pretend it's not
happening, not existing.

00:22:00.569 --> 00:22:02.362
I don't know, I'm just
too socially conscious

00:22:02.362 --> 00:22:06.700
and I feel like I had a fight to get here

00:22:06.700 --> 00:22:09.578
and I've earned the scars and the wounds.

00:22:09.578 --> 00:22:13.332
And I wanna talk about how
I had to fight to get here.

00:22:13.332 --> 00:22:15.876
If anybody had done what I
had done, they'd be so much

00:22:15.876 --> 00:22:16.710
further.

00:22:16.710 --> 00:22:19.004
I mean I got a producer,
I'm not gonna name his name.

00:22:19.004 --> 00:22:20.589
But he said, you know, "Too
bad John you're Puerto Rican,

00:22:20.589 --> 00:22:21.423
'cause you're so talented.

00:22:21.423 --> 00:22:22.883
Otherwise you'd be so much further along."

00:22:22.883 --> 00:22:26.094
And that wasn't an insult,
it was an actual fact.

00:22:26.094 --> 00:22:27.846
At the time I didn't know how to take it,

00:22:27.846 --> 00:22:30.140
but now I know it's like, yeah,

00:22:30.140 --> 00:22:32.726
because you were Latin,
you only gonna get so far.

00:22:32.726 --> 00:22:34.186
You're only gonna get certain roles.

00:22:34.186 --> 00:22:35.812
You're only gonna get
certain opportunities.

00:22:35.812 --> 00:22:39.149
I was in a movie for a week

00:22:39.149 --> 00:22:40.650
and then I get a call
from the director going,

00:22:40.650 --> 00:22:41.568
"Oh John, I'm sorry,

00:22:41.568 --> 00:22:42.861
I can't have you in the movie anymore."

00:22:42.861 --> 00:22:43.987
I go, why?

00:22:43.987 --> 00:22:45.572
What happened?

00:22:45.572 --> 00:22:46.573
You lose the funding?

00:22:46.573 --> 00:22:48.909
"No, no, we cast this Latin actress

00:22:48.909 --> 00:22:50.994
and we can't have two
Latin people in the movie

00:22:50.994 --> 00:22:52.704
because it becomes a Latin movie.

00:22:52.704 --> 00:22:53.705
You know what I mean right?"

00:22:53.705 --> 00:22:55.499
And I'm like, you mean...

00:22:55.499 --> 00:22:56.958
And I'm like, I wanted to say like,

00:22:56.958 --> 00:22:58.335
what do you mean it's better?

00:22:58.335 --> 00:23:01.630
No, no he meant like I can
only have one token at a time-

00:23:01.630 --> 00:23:02.464
- And this recent?

00:23:02.464 --> 00:23:04.341
- No, this was about eight years ago.

00:23:05.175 --> 00:23:08.845
I mean, tokenism is real,
glass ceilings are real,

00:23:08.845 --> 00:23:11.181
colorblind casting was promised to me

00:23:11.181 --> 00:23:14.267
by all the artists in the 70s,

00:23:14.267 --> 00:23:15.685
we were gonna break that down

00:23:15.685 --> 00:23:18.563
and then it wasn't until Hamilton,

00:23:18.563 --> 00:23:21.650
2000 somewhat that they finally did it.

00:23:21.650 --> 00:23:23.985
And it succeeded, it
showed that it does work.

00:23:23.985 --> 00:23:26.571
- Take me to you going back to theater

00:23:26.571 --> 00:23:28.824
for "Latin History For Morons."

00:23:28.824 --> 00:23:30.659
I wanna talk about what I imagined

00:23:30.659 --> 00:23:33.036
was probably a very personal conversation

00:23:33.036 --> 00:23:34.079
between you and your son.

00:23:34.079 --> 00:23:35.163
- Right.

00:23:35.163 --> 00:23:36.706
- Can you take me to that day
when he comes home from school

00:23:36.706 --> 00:23:38.041
and he tells you that he's being bullied?

00:23:38.041 --> 00:23:39.459
Like what was that conversation like?

00:23:39.459 --> 00:23:41.294
- That's kind of hard, especially

00:23:41.294 --> 00:23:43.338
after having some mescal, I don't know.

00:23:43.338 --> 00:23:45.715
I don't wanna get mad emotional on camera.

00:23:45.715 --> 00:23:50.720
I don't wanna, I wanna keep this...

00:23:53.140 --> 00:23:54.349
It's a hard conversation obviously.

00:23:54.349 --> 00:23:58.103
You know, it's your beloved child

00:23:58.103 --> 00:24:02.315
and they're being mistreated
and profiled racially

00:24:02.315 --> 00:24:05.986
and ostracized and the school
didn't know how to handle it.

00:24:05.986 --> 00:24:06.987
And it was terrible.

00:24:09.406 --> 00:24:10.657
But I wanted to be an involved father,

00:24:10.657 --> 00:24:11.950
I didn't wanna be like my dad,

00:24:11.950 --> 00:24:14.536
who would we go, you know, go kick his,

00:24:14.536 --> 00:24:15.662
don't come back home to kick his ass.

00:24:15.662 --> 00:24:17.831
The door's locked to
you, 'til kick his ass.

00:24:17.831 --> 00:24:19.458
I mean, I wanted to do that,

00:24:19.458 --> 00:24:21.293
but I also wanted to be an evolved dad.

00:24:21.293 --> 00:24:23.753
And I wanted to weaponize his information

00:24:23.753 --> 00:24:26.631
and give him Latin
information about our history

00:24:26.631 --> 00:24:29.301
to strengthen him, to arm him.

00:24:29.301 --> 00:24:30.594
I looked through a history textbook

00:24:30.594 --> 00:24:31.761
and it's like, wait a minute,

00:24:31.761 --> 00:24:35.807
there's nothing mentioning
our contributions or anything.

00:24:35.807 --> 00:24:37.726
It's like we didn't exist.

00:24:37.726 --> 00:24:40.061
So I started doing mad
research and learning so much.

00:24:40.061 --> 00:24:42.981
And the crazy thing was I
was the one that was healing.

00:24:42.981 --> 00:24:45.775
I was the one that was being empowered.

00:24:45.775 --> 00:24:46.651
And then I realized, oh my God,

00:24:46.651 --> 00:24:50.906
I can do this for the 70
million Latinos in America.

00:24:50.906 --> 00:24:53.783
I can do this maybe for
the 500 million speakers

00:24:53.783 --> 00:24:57.245
in the world, through this work.

00:24:57.245 --> 00:25:00.373
- Let me take it back
just a little bit to-

00:25:00.373 --> 00:25:02.417
- You go wherever you wanna
go, this is your show.

00:25:02.417 --> 00:25:03.293
- To the conversation-

00:25:03.293 --> 00:25:06.004
- I'm not tryna hack up the airways bro.

00:25:06.004 --> 00:25:07.923
- I know you don't
wanna get too emotional,

00:25:07.923 --> 00:25:10.967
I just wanna talk a little
bit more on the personal side.

00:25:10.967 --> 00:25:12.677
Cause it's like everyone
can turn on Netflix

00:25:12.677 --> 00:25:13.678
and see the result of it.

00:25:13.678 --> 00:25:14.679
- Right.

00:25:14.679 --> 00:25:16.348
- But the fact that it came
from such personal caring,

00:25:16.348 --> 00:25:18.600
from one generation to the
next generation of like,

00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:20.060
I don't want you to go
through what I went through.

00:25:20.060 --> 00:25:21.603
- No.

00:25:21.603 --> 00:25:23.522
I don't want any kid to go
through what I went through.

00:25:23.522 --> 00:25:25.440
I don't want any Latin kid to go through

00:25:25.440 --> 00:25:26.316
what I went through.

00:25:26.316 --> 00:25:28.568
I don't want any white
kid, black kid, Asian kid.

00:25:28.568 --> 00:25:31.446
And for it to be so close
to home, it was devastating.

00:25:31.446 --> 00:25:34.699
So I was definitely
couldn't believe that my son

00:25:34.699 --> 00:25:39.454
is having to go through the
same shit 40 years later,

00:25:39.454 --> 00:25:42.165
that I went through his crazy.

00:25:42.165 --> 00:25:44.751
- So that conversation
happens at what point did,

00:25:44.751 --> 00:25:46.336
does it instantly hit you?

00:25:46.336 --> 00:25:48.171
- Oh my God he will not let it go.

00:25:48.171 --> 00:25:49.881
(man laughs) Oh my God
he will not let it go.

00:25:49.881 --> 00:25:50.840
You can tell he is Latin,

00:25:50.840 --> 00:25:53.218
and Latin people are tenacious as (beep)

00:25:53.218 --> 00:25:54.052
(man laughs)

00:25:54.052 --> 00:25:54.886
Holy shit.

00:25:54.886 --> 00:25:55.720
(man laughs)

00:25:55.720 --> 00:25:56.096
(people chattering)

00:25:56.096 --> 00:25:58.098
(in Spanish)
I understand but we’re talking

00:25:58.098 --> 00:26:00.016
(in Spanish)
about what I want to talk about.

00:26:00.016 --> 00:26:01.810
We're gonna talk about
what I wanna talk about.

00:26:01.810 --> 00:26:03.311
You can skirt the issue you want,

00:26:03.311 --> 00:26:05.105
but I'm still getting back.

00:26:05.105 --> 00:26:06.940
That is the reporter in his mom.

00:26:06.940 --> 00:26:07.774
Let's talk about your mom.

00:26:07.774 --> 00:26:09.067
(man laughs)

00:26:09.067 --> 00:26:10.318
I wanna talk about your mom
because your mom's incredible,

00:26:10.318 --> 00:26:12.362
she was a big influence on you.

00:26:12.362 --> 00:26:14.573
No, no, don't try to skirt the issue.

00:26:14.573 --> 00:26:16.116
- I'm gonna bring it back around.

00:26:16.116 --> 00:26:17.534
I'm tied up with a nice knot.

00:26:17.534 --> 00:26:19.578
You brought up my mom,
my mom's a professor in-

00:26:19.578 --> 00:26:21.538
- In a good way, out of respect.

00:26:21.538 --> 00:26:22.747
- In an amazing way.

00:26:22.747 --> 00:26:24.416
My mom's a professor at Berkeley.

00:26:24.416 --> 00:26:25.750
She comes in and stays with me,

00:26:25.750 --> 00:26:28.003
it probably was for
Thanksgiving or Christmas.

00:26:28.003 --> 00:26:30.130
I show her "Latin History For Morons."

00:26:30.130 --> 00:26:31.673
She's like this is amazing.

00:26:31.673 --> 00:26:32.674
(man laughs)

00:26:32.674 --> 00:26:34.301
The next semester she
incorporates it into the class.

00:26:34.301 --> 00:26:36.136
- Oh no get out its amazing.
- Yeah.

00:26:36.136 --> 00:26:37.178
- That's incredible.

00:26:37.178 --> 00:26:38.680
But your mom was a huge influence for you.

00:26:38.680 --> 00:26:39.639
And-

00:26:39.639 --> 00:26:41.266
- My mom's my hero.

00:26:41.266 --> 00:26:42.684
Like I didn't turn on a TV to,

00:26:42.684 --> 00:26:44.853
there wasn't anything on TV for me.

00:26:44.853 --> 00:26:46.062
Like you said, there wasn't really much

00:26:46.062 --> 00:26:47.314
for me to turn on and be
like, oh, this is something,

00:26:47.314 --> 00:26:49.858
like my mom was my mom,
my dad, my best friend.

00:26:49.858 --> 00:26:51.526
Like she wore all the hats.

00:26:51.526 --> 00:26:53.653
- Yeah, the single Latin single mom.

00:26:53.653 --> 00:26:55.030
Yeah.

00:26:55.030 --> 00:26:56.031
- Yeah.

00:26:56.031 --> 00:26:57.699
My mom was a single mom
telling me I had a dad until,

00:26:57.699 --> 00:26:58.867
I mean, I still have a dad,

00:26:58.867 --> 00:27:01.786
but he was at home until we were 13.

00:27:01.786 --> 00:27:04.331
And then my mom became
a supermom, single mom,

00:27:04.331 --> 00:27:05.790
Latin mom, dad.

00:27:05.790 --> 00:27:07.208
- So you know.

00:27:07.208 --> 00:27:08.460
- Yeah but we're the
same flavor, you know,

00:27:08.460 --> 00:27:10.670
like sort of urban street intellectuals.

00:27:10.670 --> 00:27:12.339
We're educated, we made it through college

00:27:12.339 --> 00:27:13.798
because of our single moms and whatnot.

00:27:13.798 --> 00:27:15.884
And it's a certain badge of honor.

00:27:15.884 --> 00:27:16.843
- I wear that proudly.

00:27:16.843 --> 00:27:17.677
- Yeah, yeah.

00:27:17.677 --> 00:27:18.595
Me too, me too.

00:27:18.595 --> 00:27:19.429
Here's to you.

00:27:19.429 --> 00:27:20.263
- Here's to you

00:27:20.263 --> 00:27:21.264
- Once again

00:27:21.264 --> 00:27:22.140
(man laughs)

00:27:22.140 --> 00:27:24.643
(calm music)

00:27:29.105 --> 00:27:31.232
- What's left for you to accomplish?

00:27:31.232 --> 00:27:32.192
- Oh, so much.

00:27:32.192 --> 00:27:37.072
I love to help the next generation
get their work done, made,

00:27:37.072 --> 00:27:37.947
produced.

00:27:37.947 --> 00:27:39.532
There's so much great talent out there.

00:27:39.532 --> 00:27:41.618
I wanna be able to help them.

00:27:41.618 --> 00:27:45.664
I wanna be that studio exec
that gets their material

00:27:45.664 --> 00:27:47.999
and their product greenlit.

00:27:47.999 --> 00:27:50.669
- The exec we've been
needing for a long time.

00:27:50.669 --> 00:27:51.670
I love it.

00:27:51.670 --> 00:27:52.504
To you.

00:27:52.504 --> 00:27:54.547
- No, no, you don't mean,
to you bro. (man laughs)

00:27:54.547 --> 00:27:55.423
Here's to you again.

00:27:55.423 --> 00:27:56.257
(man laughs)

00:27:56.257 --> 00:27:57.092
- Let's stop this...

00:27:57.092 --> 00:27:58.760
- No, no, no. You know,
you're a narcissist come on.

00:27:58.760 --> 00:28:00.428
It's all about him.

00:28:00.428 --> 00:28:02.097
Come on.
- This one is to you.

00:28:02.097 --> 00:28:04.140
- No, no, you don't have
to do it. Don't even try.

00:28:04.140 --> 00:28:05.225
(in Spanish)
To culture.

00:28:05.225 --> 00:28:06.393
Let's go.

00:28:06.393 --> 00:28:07.227
(in Spanish)
To culture.

00:28:07.227 --> 00:28:08.061
- Much love, much love.

00:28:08.061 --> 00:28:08.937
Much respect.

00:28:08.937 --> 00:28:09.771
- Likewise.

00:28:09.771 --> 00:28:12.440
(upbeat music)

00:28:22.158 --> 00:28:23.910
So we're on the way back to the hotel.

00:28:23.910 --> 00:28:25.870
As you can tell, we're in
a New York City street,

00:28:25.870 --> 00:28:28.248
we got jackhammers, taxi cabs,

00:28:28.248 --> 00:28:29.666
people walking by probably wondering

00:28:29.666 --> 00:28:32.001
why I'm holding a camera, but yeah.

00:28:32.001 --> 00:28:34.671
Taking a little time to reflect
on the conversation today.

00:28:34.671 --> 00:28:37.507
I think the high school senior
in me is still freaking out

00:28:37.507 --> 00:28:41.553
that I got to meet somebody
who inspired me greatly.

00:28:41.553 --> 00:28:44.556
I am thankful for John
for opening up his home

00:28:44.556 --> 00:28:46.349
and just being thoughtful

00:28:46.349 --> 00:28:47.809
and sharing a very vulnerable side to him.

00:28:47.809 --> 00:28:49.728
I think it's important you know,

00:28:49.728 --> 00:28:53.231
we hear numbers thrown
around 70 million Latinos,

00:28:53.231 --> 00:28:54.983
4% of movie roles.

00:28:54.983 --> 00:28:57.026
But I think when you see
the humanity behind it,

00:28:57.026 --> 00:28:58.194
when you see how it affects a son,

00:28:58.194 --> 00:29:00.071
when you see how it affects a father,

00:29:00.071 --> 00:29:01.364
I think that's when it's like,

00:29:01.364 --> 00:29:04.033
okay, now I understand what
this conversation is about.

00:29:04.033 --> 00:29:06.202
And I'm thankful for John for doing that.

00:29:06.202 --> 00:29:09.080
And it's cold and I'm looking
forward to the next episode.

00:29:09.080 --> 00:29:10.707
So thank you for tuning in

00:29:10.707 --> 00:29:12.292
and we will see you at the next one.

00:29:12.292 --> 00:29:14.919
(upbeat music)

